"""Move the blog prose out of the React bundle and into the database (audit Z3-12).

`BlogPost.jsx` carried ~700 lines of article prose as a hardcoded object and
preferred it over the API whenever the stored body was under 200 characters. The
earlier seed migration (0006) inserted only three posts, each a short stub — which
is *why* that override existed, and why editing a post in the admin appeared to do
nothing: a real edit shorter than the bundled prose was silently discarded.

Five of the eight posts existed only in the bundle, so deleting it would have
404'd them. This migration makes the database genuinely authoritative by writing
the full text of all eight.

Deliberately does NOT overwrite a post whose stored content is already longer than
the bundled copy. If someone has edited a post in the admin since 0006 ran, their
work is the newer truth and a migration must not silently revert it — the whole
point of this change is that the database wins.

Reversible: the reverse operation is a no-op rather than a delete. Rolling back a
data migration should not destroy content an editor may have since changed.
"""

from django.db import migrations
from django.utils import timezone

